// 42 个主要国家的人文历史地理介绍（英文）
window.COUNTRY_STORIES_EN = {
  "156": {
    tags: ["Ancient civilization", "Multi-ethnic nation", "World's 2nd largest economy"],
    geo: "Located in eastern Asia on the western Pacific, China covers about 9.6 million square kilometres, the third largest area in the world. Its terrain descends in three steps from the Tibetan Plateau in the west through plateaus and basins to broad alluvial plains in the east. The Yangtze and Yellow Rivers flow eastward into the Pacific, nourishing Chinese civilisation. Climates range from tropical to cold temperate, with a pronounced monsoon and great biodiversity.",
    hist: "With over five thousand years of civilisation, oracle bones and bronze ware bear witness to the Shang and Zhou dynasties. Qin unification established centralised rule and the commandery system, followed by the prosperity of Han and Tang, the economic and cultural flowering of Song and Yuan, and the transitions of Ming and Qing. After 1840 came a century of upheaval; the People's Republic was founded in 1949. Since reform and opening-up, China has become a global manufacturing and trade hub.",
    cult: "Confucianism, Buddhism and Daoism together shaped ethics and aesthetics; Chinese characters, calligraphy, painting, Peking opera and Kunqu are unique. Cuisine is rich with regional schools such as Sichuan, Shandong, Cantonese and Jiangsu. Festivals like Spring Festival, Mid-Autumn and Dragon Boat sustain family ties. Fifty-six ethnic groups coexist, and the Great Wall, Forbidden City and Terracotta Warriors testify to a profound heritage.",
    facts: [["Area", "9.6M km²"], ["Population", "~1.4B"], ["Capital", "Beijing"], ["Currency", "Renminbi"], ["Language", "Chinese"]]
  },
  "840": {
    tags: ["Nation of immigrants", "Superpower", "Federal republic"],
    geo: "The mainland lies in central North America, bordered by Canada and Mexico and flanked by the Atlantic and Pacific; Alaska sits in the northwest and Hawaii in the Pacific. Covering about 9.37 million square kilometres, the land forms north-south bands: the western Cordillera, the central plains and the low eastern Appalachians. The Mississippi is the largest river and the Great Lakes the world's biggest freshwater lake group.",
    hist: "Indigenous peoples lived here for tens of thousands of years. European colonists founded thirteen colonies in the early 17th century; the Declaration of Independence came in 1776 and the Constitution in 1787. The 19th century brought vast territorial expansion and the Civil War abolished slavery. Rising through two world wars, the US became a superpower and has led global politics, economy, technology and military affairs since the Cold War.",
    cult: "Known as a 'melting pot', immigration blends many cultures. Hollywood, jazz, rock and hip-hop shape global popular culture; individualism and innovation are core values, with Silicon Valley driving the IT revolution. Thanksgiving and Independence Day are major holidays, while cowboys, basketball and baseball are enduring cultural symbols.",
    facts: [["Area", "9.37M km²"], ["Population", "~330M"], ["Capital", "Washington, D.C."], ["Currency", "US Dollar"], ["Language", "English"]]
  },
  "643": {
    tags: ["Spanning Europe & Asia", "Largest by area", "Resource-rich"],
    geo: "The world's largest country, about 17.09 million square kilometres, stretches across eastern Europe and northern Asia, facing the Arctic and Pacific Oceans. The Urals, Ural River and Caucasus roughly divide Europe from Asia. Plains and lowlands dominate; Siberian permafrost is widespread and the Volga is revered as the 'mother river'. Lake Baikal is the world's deepest, and forests, oil, gas and mineral reserves rank among the largest globally.",
    hist: "Kievan Rus was the early state; after the 13th-century Mongol invasion it fragmented and the Grand Duchy of Moscow rose. Ivan IV was crowned tsar in 1547 and the Romanovs expanded further. The 1917 October Revolution created the world's first socialist state, the USSR, a superpower after WWII. In 1991 the Soviet Union dissolved and the Russian Federation inherited its main legacy as a federal republic.",
    cult: "Russian literature holds a major place in world letters—Tolstoy, Dostoevsky and Pushkin are hugely influential. Tchaikovsky's music and the ballet Swan Lake are renowned. Orthodox Christianity runs deep, with onion-domed churches and Red Square as icons. Vodka, matryoshka dolls, Russian poetry and long winters shape a distinctive national character.",
    facts: [["Area", "17.09M km²"], ["Population", "~144M"], ["Capital", "Moscow"], ["Currency", "Ruble"], ["Language", "Russian"]]
  },
  "392": {
    tags: ["Island nation", "Constitutional monarchy", "Tech powerhouse"],
    geo: "An island country in the Pacific off East Asia, made up of Hokkaido, Honshu, Shikoku, Kyushu and thousands of smaller islands, with about 378,000 square kilometres. Mountains and hills cover roughly three quarters; volcanoes are numerous along the Pacific Ring of Fire, bringing frequent earthquakes and typhoons. Mount Fuji, an active volcano, symbolises the nation. The indented coast offers many fine harbours, and marine and fishery resources are rich.",
    hist: "The Yamato state unified Japan by the 3rd century; the Taika Reform of 645 established centralised rule. Nara and Heian periods were deeply influenced by Tang and Song China. From the 12th century, samurai rule and shogunate governance lasted nearly seven centuries. The 1868 Meiji Restoration modernised and industrialised the country, which later expanded abroad. After WWII it was reformed under occupation, regained sovereignty in 1951 and grew into a major economy.",
    cult: "Shinto and Buddhism coexist, with the emperor as a symbolic head. Tea ceremony, flower arrangement, calligraphy and judo emphasise ritual and practice. Ukiyo-e, anime, film and pop music influence the world. Sushi and ramen are globally loved, while cherry blossoms, kimono, shrines and distinct seasons form a refined aesthetic sensibility.",
    facts: [["Area", "378K km²"], ["Population", "~124M"], ["Capital", "Tokyo"], ["Currency", "Yen"], ["Language", "Japanese"]]
  },
  "826": {
    tags: ["Island nation", "Constitutional monarchy", "Birthplace of industry"],
    geo: "Off northwest Europe in the Atlantic, the UK comprises Great Britain, northeast Ireland and many smaller islands, covering about 244,000 square kilometres. The terrain varies: Scotland is mountainous with lochs, the midlands and south are rolling hills and plains, and Wales is hilly. The Thames is the chief river. A temperate maritime climate brings year-round rain, mild winters and cool summers; fog once symbolised London.",
    hist: "Rome ruled Britain for nearly four centuries, then Anglo-Saxons arrived in the 5th century. The Norman Conquest of 1066 forged a unified kingdom. Magna Carta in the 13th century laid the rule-of-law tradition and the Glorious Revolution of 1688 established constitutional monarchy. The 18th century saw the Industrial Revolution and a vast empire. After two world wars the empire faded, yet the UK remains a UN Security Council permanent member and a key financial centre.",
    cult: "Shakespeare, Dickens and Jane Austen shaped world literature; the Beatles and Rolling Stones transformed modern music. The home of modern football, the Premier League is famed worldwide. Afternoon tea, gentlemanly manners, parliamentary debate and country houses form classic imagery. London, Oxford and Cambridge combine deep history with academic prestige.",
    facts: [["Area", "244K km²"], ["Population", "~68M"], ["Capital", "London"], ["Currency", "Pound Sterling"], ["Language", "English"]]
  },
  "250": {
    tags: ["Hexagonal nation", "Republic", "Cultural powerhouse"],
    geo: "In Western Europe, France's territory is roughly hexagonal, about 552,000 square kilometres, facing Britain across the Channel and the Mediterranean to the southeast. Plains and plateaus dominate, with the Pyrenees in the southwest and the Alps in the southeast. The Seine, Loire and Rhône cross the land. Its climate blends oceanic, Mediterranean and continental traits, and its agriculture and wine regions are world-famous.",
    hist: "Roman-conquered Gaul gradually romanised, then Franks founded a kingdom in the 5th century. The Capetians from 987 strengthened royal power. The Hundred Years' War and religious wars shaped the nation in the late Middle Ages. The reign of Louis XIV marked the height of royal and court culture. The 1789 Revolution overthrew the old order and spread liberty and equality; industrialisation and empire followed, and the Fifth Republic was founded after WWII.",
    cult: "French thought, literature, fashion and cuisine exert wide influence. The Eiffel Tower, Louvre and Notre-Dame are iconic; cinema, haute couture and wine are global benchmarks. Bastille Day is the national holiday. Philosophers such as Voltaire and Rousseau shaped modern ideas, and the country remains a centre of art, gastronomy and diplomacy.",
    facts: [["Area", "552K km²"], ["Population", "~68M"], ["Capital", "Paris"], ["Currency", "Euro"], ["Language", "French"]]
  },
  "276": {
    tags: ["Central European power", "Federal republic", "Engineering nation"],
    geo: "Situated in Central Europe, Germany spans about 358,000 square kilometres from the North Sea and Baltic in the north to the Alpine foothills in the south. The Rhine, Elbe and Danube are major rivers. Northern plains give way to central uplands and southern mountains. A temperate climate with warm summers and cold winters supports dense forests and a highly developed transport network.",
    hist: "Germanic tribes long inhabited the region; the Frankish empire and later the Holy Roman Empire shaped its politics. The 16th-century Reformation, led by Luther, reshaped Europe. Unification came in 1871 under Prussia, followed by rapid industrialisation. Defeat in two world wars and the Cold War division into East and West ended with reunification in 1990. Today it is a leading EU member and economic powerhouse.",
    cult: "A land of poets and thinkers—Goethe, Schiller, Kant and Nietzsche—Germany is renowned for classical music (Bach, Beethoven) and philosophical thought. Oktoberfest, Christmas markets and a strong brewing tradition are treasured. Precision engineering, the Autobahn and innovative design reflect a culture of order and craftsmanship.",
    facts: [["Area", "358K km²"], ["Population", "~84M"], ["Capital", "Berlin"], ["Currency", "Euro"], ["Language", "German"]]
  },
  "356": {
    tags: ["Ancient civilisation", "Most populous", "Diverse subcontinent"],
    geo: "Occupying most of the Indian subcontinent in South Asia, India covers about 3.29 million square kilometres. The Himalayas rise in the north, the Indo-Gangetic plains lie in the centre and the Deccan Plateau extends south, flanked by the Western and Eastern Ghats. The Ganges and Indus are sacred rivers. Climates range from tropical monsoon to alpine, supporting rich wildlife and fertile farmland.",
    hist: "The Indus Valley civilisation flourished before 1500 BCE, followed by the Vedic age and the rise of Buddhism and Jainism. Magadha and the Maurya and Gupta empires unified large areas; the classical 'Golden Age' advanced science and art. Medieval sultanates and the Mughal Empire left grand architecture. British colonial rule from the 18th century ended with independence and partition in 1947, after which India became a republic in 1950.",
    cult: "Hinduism, Islam, Buddhism, Sikhism and Jainism coexist across a remarkably diverse society. Hindi and English are widely used alongside many regional languages. Indian cuisine,yoga, Bollywood cinema and classical dance are globally known. Festivals such as Diwali, Holi and Eid are celebrated vibrantly, and the Taj Mahal stands as a world heritage icon.",
    facts: [["Area", "3.29M km²"], ["Population", "~1.43B"], ["Capital", "New Delhi"], ["Currency", "Indian Rupee"], ["Language", "Hindi, English"]]
  },
  "076": {
    tags: ["Largest in South America", "Amazon rainforest", "Sporting nation"],
    geo: "Brazil occupies nearly half of South America, about 8.51 million square kilometres, bordering most of the continent and facing the Atlantic. The Amazon basin in the north holds the world's largest rainforest and mightiest river; the northeast is semi-arid, while the south has rolling highlands and fertile plains. A tropical climate dominates, with immense biodiversity and vast water resources.",
    hist: "Indigenous peoples lived across the land before Portugal claimed it in 1500 under the Treaty of Tordesillas. Colonial rule lasted three centuries, with sugar, gold and coffee economies and the transatlantic slave trade. Independence came in 1822 under the House of Braganza; a republic was founded in 1889. Military rule from 1964 gave way to democracy in 1985, and Brazil is now a major emerging economy.",
    cult: "Samba, bossa nova and carnival express a vibrant national spirit; football is almost a religion, with five World Cup titles. Brazilian barbecue, coffee and the Amazonian heritage enrich its culture. Rio de Janeiro's beaches and Christ the Redeemer are iconic, while the country's ethnic mix—Portuguese, African, Indigenous and immigrant—creates a warm, diverse society.",
    facts: [["Area", "8.51M km²"], ["Population", "~213M"], ["Capital", "Brasília"], ["Currency", "Real"], ["Language", "Portuguese"]]
  },
  "036": {
    tags: ["Island continent", "Southern hemisphere", "Natural wonders"],
    geo: "Australia is both a country and the smallest continent, covering about 7.69 million square kilometres between the Indian and Pacific Oceans. The interior is largely arid desert and semi-desert, ringed by fertile coastal plains; the Great Dividing Range runs along the east. The Murray-Darling is the main river system. Unique wildlife—kangaroos, koalas and the Great Barrier Reef—reflect ancient isolation.",
    hist: "Aboriginal peoples have lived here for over 50,000 years, maintaining rich cultures. Dutch and Portuguese explorers sighted the land; Captain Cook claimed the east for Britain in 1770. Convict settlements began in 1788, followed by free settlers and the gold rushes of the 1850s. The six colonies federated in 1901 as the Commonwealth of Australia, gradually gaining independence within the Commonwealth.",
    cult: "A relaxed outdoor lifestyle centres on beaches, barbecues and sport; cricket, swimming and rugby are national passions. Sydney Opera House and the Harbour Bridge are landmarks. Aboriginal art, including dot painting and didgeridoo music, expresses one of the world's oldest continuous cultures. Multicultural immigration has shaped a welcoming, diverse society.",
    facts: [["Area", "7.69M km²"], ["Population", "~27M"], ["Capital", "Canberra"], ["Currency", "Australian Dollar"], ["Language", "English"]]
  },
  "124": {
    tags: ["Second largest by area", "Federal state", "Bilingual nation"],
    geo: "Canada occupies the northern half of North America, about 9.98 million square kilometres, the world's second largest country. It is washed by the Atlantic, Pacific and Arctic Oceans and shares a long border with the US. The Canadian Shield, vast plains and the western Cordillera define its landscapes; thousands of lakes hold much of the world's fresh water. The climate ranges from arctic to temperate, with coniferous forests across the north.",
    hist: "Indigenous First Nations, Inuit and Métis lived here for millennia. Norse voyagers arrived around 1000 CE; later French and British colonisation competed for control. The Treaty of Paris of 1763 gave Britain most of the territory. Confederation in 1867 created the Dominion of Canada, which gained full sovereignty gradually, completed by the 1982 Constitution Act.",
    cult: "English and French are official languages, and bilingualism shapes identity, especially in Quebec. Canadian identity blends Indigenous heritage, pioneer history and immigrant cultures. Ice hockey is the national winter sport; maple syrup, poutine and the Rocky Mountains are beloved symbols. Toronto, Vancouver and Montreal are vibrant multicultural cities.",
    facts: [["Area", "9.98M km²"], ["Population", "~40M"], ["Capital", "Ottawa"], ["Currency", "Canadian Dollar"], ["Language", "English, French"]]
  },
  "818": {
    tags: ["Cradle of civilisation", "Arab world", "Desert & Nile"],
    geo: "Egypt sits in northeast Africa, with the Sinai Peninsula extending into Asia, covering about 1.01 million square kilometres. The Nile flows north through the country, its valley and delta holding most of the population and fertile land; the rest is largely desert. The climate is hot and dry, with great temperature swings. The Nile, the Mediterranean and the Red Sea have sustained life and trade for millennia.",
    hist: "Egypt's Nile valley gave rise to one of the world's oldest civilisations, unified around 3100 BCE. The Old, Middle and New Kingdoms built the pyramids and temples; Tutankhamun and Ramses II are famous pharaohs. After Persian, Greek and Roman rule, Arab conquest in the 7th century brought Islam and Arabic. The Ottoman era was followed by British occupation until the 1952 revolution established a republic.",
    cult: "Egyptian culture is deeply rooted in Arab and Islamic tradition while preserving ancient heritage. The Giza pyramids, Sphinx and Luxor temples are wonders of the ancient world. Cairo is a bustling cultural centre; Egyptian cinema and music influence the Arab world. Tea, coffee and hospitality are central to daily life, and the Nile remains a symbol of national identity.",
    facts: [["Area", "1.01M km²"], ["Population", "~112M"], ["Capital", "Cairo"], ["Currency", "Egyptian Pound"], ["Language", "Arabic"]]
  },
  "710": {
    tags: ["Rainbow nation", "Mineral-rich", "Cape region"],
    geo: "At the southern tip of Africa, South Africa covers about 1.22 million square kilometres, bordered by the Atlantic and Indian Oceans. The interior is a high plateau ringed by the Drakensberg mountains, with semi-arid Karoo and fertile coastal belts. The Limpopo and Orange are major rivers. A subtropical climate and diverse ecosystems support rich wildlife and famous national parks.",
    hist: "San hunter-gatherers and Khoikhoi lived here first, followed by Bantu-speaking peoples. Dutch settlers arrived in 1652; British rule followed, and the Great Trek led to Boer republics. The discovery of diamonds and gold fuelled conflict, and the Union of 1910 entrenched segregation. Apartheid ended in 1994 with Nelson Mandela's election, ushering in a multiracial democracy known as the 'Rainbow Nation'.",
    cult: "Eleven official languages reflect remarkable diversity; Zulu, Xhosa and Afrikaans are widely spoken. Music and dance, from gumboot to kwaito, are vibrant. The country is famed for safari wildlife,Table Mountain and the Cape wine region. Rugby, cricket and football are popular, and Mandela's legacy of reconciliation is a national touchstone.",
    facts: [["Area", "1.22M km²"], ["Population", "~60M"], ["Capital", "Pretoria / Cape Town / Bloemfontein"], ["Currency", "Rand"], ["Language", "11 official languages"]]
  },
  "380": {
    tags: ["Boot-shaped peninsula", "Cradle of Renaissance", "Mediterranean"],
    geo: "Italy occupies a boot-shaped peninsula in southern Europe extending into the Mediterranean, about 302,000 square kilometres, including Sicily and Sardinia. The Alps arc across the north, the Apennines run down the peninsula, and the Po is the largest river. Mediterranean climate brings hot dry summers and mild wet winters. Volcanic landscapes, including Vesuvius and Etna, mark its geology.",
    hist: "Italy was the heart of the Roman Empire, which unified the Mediterranean and shaped law, language and architecture. After Rome's fall, the peninsula split into city-states and kingdoms, often under foreign rule. The Renaissance from the 14th century produced an explosion of art and learning. Unification was achieved in 1861; Italy became a republic in 1946 after WWII.",
    cult: "Italy is synonymous with art, architecture and design—Leonardo, Michelangelo and Raphael define the Renaissance. Venice, Florence and Rome are treasure houses; pizza, pasta, espresso and wine are global icons. Opera and fashion are celebrated, and family and regional identity remain strong. The Vatican City, an independent state within Rome, is the centre of Catholicism.",
    facts: [["Area", "302K km²"], ["Population", "~59M"], ["Capital", "Rome"], ["Currency", "Euro"], ["Language", "Italian"]]
  },
  "724": {
    tags: ["Iberian peninsula", "Mediterranean climate", "Flamenco & football"],
    geo: "Spain occupies most of the Iberian Peninsula in southwest Europe, about 506,000 square kilometres, plus the Balearic and Canary Islands. A central plateau, the Meseta, dominates, ringed by mountains such as the Pyrenees and Sierra Nevada. The Tagus, Ebro and Guadalquivir are major rivers. Climates vary from Mediterranean along the coast to continental inland, supporting diverse landscapes.",
    hist: "Iberia saw Phoenician, Greek, Roman and Visigothic peoples. Muslim conquest in 711 brought centuries of Islamic rule and a flourishing culture. The Reconquista culminated in 1492 with the fall of Granada and Columbus's voyage, launching a global empire. The 18th-century Bourbon reforms modernised the state; the 20th century saw civil war, Franco's dictatorship and a return to democracy in the late 1970s.",
    cult: "Flamenco, Spanish guitar and festivals like La Tomatina and Running of the Bulls reflect a passionate culture. Football, especially Real Madrid and Barcelona, is world-famous. Tapas, paella and jamón are celebrated foods; siestas and late-night social life shape daily rhythm. The Alhambra, Gaudí's architecture and sunny coasts draw visitors worldwide.",
    facts: [["Area", "506K km²"], ["Population", "~48M"], ["Capital", "Madrid"], ["Currency", "Euro"], ["Language", "Spanish"]]
  },
  "410": {
    tags: ["Peninsula nation", "Rapid development", "K-wave culture"],
    geo: "South Korea occupies the southern half of the Korean Peninsula in East Asia, about 100,000 square kilometres, surrounded by the Yellow, East and Japan Seas. Mountains and hills cover most of the land, with narrow coastal plains in the west and south; the Han and Nakdong are chief rivers. A temperate monsoon climate brings hot humid summers and cold dry winters, with four distinct seasons.",
    hist: "The peninsula saw the Three Kingdoms—Goguryeo, Baekje and Silla—before Goryeo unified it in 918 and Joseon followed in 1392. Confucianism shaped society for centuries. In the late 19th century it faced foreign pressure; Japan's colonial rule from 1910 lasted until 1945, when the peninsula was divided. The Korean War (1950-53) fixed the division, after which South Korea built a democracy and a dynamic economy.",
    cult: "Confucian traditions coexist with vibrant modern culture. K-pop, Korean drama and film have swept the globe as the 'Korean Wave'. Kimchi, bibimbap and Korean barbecue are beloved; hanbok and traditional festivals like Chuseok remain important. Seoul is a high-tech metropolis where palaces and skyscrapers stand side by side.",
    facts: [["Area", "100K km²"], ["Population", "~51M"], ["Capital", "Seoul"], ["Currency", "Won"], ["Language", "Korean"]]
  },
  "704": {
    tags: ["S-shaped coast", "River delta", "Transforming economy"],
    geo: "Vietnam lies on the eastern Indochina Peninsula, about 331,000 square kilometres, with a long S-shaped coastline along the South China Sea. The north is mountainous and the Red River delta fertile; the south is the vast Mekong delta, while the Annamite Range runs along the west. A tropical monsoon climate brings hot wet summers and mild dry winters. Rivers and deltas sustain rice cultivation and a rich aquatic ecosystem.",
    hist: "Vietnamese civilisation began in the Red River delta; the Ly and Tran dynasties built a strong state and resisted Mongol invasions. The Nguyen lords expanded southward. France colonised Indochina in the 19th century; after WWII the Viet Minh fought for independence, culminating in the 1954 Geneva Accords and later the Vietnam War. Reunification came in 1976, and Đổi Mới reforms from 1986 spurred rapid growth.",
    cult: "Confucian, Buddhist and Taoist influences blend with local beliefs. Ancestral worship and family ties are important. Phở, bánh mì and coffee are beloved; the áo dài is a graceful national garment. Water puppetry, lacquerware and festivals like Tết reflect a rich heritage. Halong Bay, with its limestone islets, is a natural wonder.",
    facts: [["Area", "331K km²"], ["Population", "~100M"], ["Capital", "Hanoi"], ["Currency", "Dong"], ["Language", "Vietnamese"]]
  },
  "764": {
    tags: ["Land of smiles", "Buddhist kingdom", "Tropical monsoon"],
    geo: "Thailand lies in the centre of Indochina, about 513,000 square kilometres, bordered by Myanmar, Laos, Cambodia and Malaysia. The north and west are mountainous, the northeast is a plateau, and the Chao Phraya river basin in the centre is a fertile rice plain; the south extends as a peninsula. A tropical monsoon climate brings hot weather, a rainy season and cool dry months.",
    hist: "Early kingdoms such as Dvaravati and Srivijaya flourished before the rise of Sukhothai in the 13th century, often called the golden age of Thai culture. Ayutthaya followed as a powerful trading kingdom. The Chakri dynasty founded Bangkok in 1782 and modernised the realm. Absolute monarchy ended in 1932, after which Thailand became a constitutional monarchy, the only Southeast Asian nation never colonised by a European power.",
    cult: "Theravada Buddhism is central, with glowing temples and a monastic tradition. The 'Land of Smiles' is known for warm hospitality. Thai cuisine—tom yum, pad thai and green curry—is globally loved; the traditional massage, Muay Thai boxing and colorful festivals like Songkran are distinctive. Bangkok's Grand Palace and northern hill tribes add to its charm.",
    facts: [["Area", "513K km²"], ["Population", "~72M"], ["Capital", "Bangkok"], ["Currency", "Baht"], ["Language", "Thai"]]
  },
  "702": {
    tags: ["Special Administrative Region", "Global financial hub", "East Asian gateway"],
    geo: "Hong Kong is a Special Administrative Region of China on the Pearl River Delta, comprising Hong Kong Island, Kowloon and the New Territories with over 260 islands, about 1,100 square kilometres. Hilly terrain and a deep natural harbour define it; Victoria Harbour is one of the world's finest. A subtropical monsoon climate brings hot humid summers and mild winters.",
    hist: "A fishing and trading area under successive dynasties, Hong Kong became a British colony in 1842 after the Opium War and grew into a major entrepôt. It developed into a global financial and trade centre in the 20th century. In 1997 sovereignty was handed back to China under 'one country, two systems', becoming a Special Administrative Region with a high degree of autonomy.",
    cult: "A fusion of Cantonese Chinese and Western influences shapes a cosmopolitan identity. Skyscrapers, trams, dim sum and tea houses coexist with temples and traditional festivals. It is a global financial, logistics and creative hub; the star ferry, Victoria Peak and vibrant street life are iconic.",
    facts: [["Area", "~1,100 km²"], ["Population", "~7.4M"], ["Capital", "—"], ["Currency", "Hong Kong Dollar"], ["Language", "Chinese, English"]]
  },
  "360": {
    tags: ["Archipelagic nation", "Largest Muslim population", "Volcanic islands"],
    geo: "Indonesia is an archipelago of over 17,000 islands between the Indian and Pacific Oceans, spanning about 1.9 million square kilometres. Sumatra, Java, Borneo, Sulawesi and Papua are the main islands. Lying on the Pacific Ring of Fire, it has many active volcanoes and frequent earthquakes; fertile volcanic soils support dense populations, especially on Java. A tropical rainforest climate brings high heat and rainfall year-round.",
    hist: "Hindu-Buddhist kingdoms such as Srivijaya and Majapahit flourished and left grand temples like Borobudur. Islam spread from the 13th century, and the Dutch East India Company established colonial rule that lasted until the 20th century. Independence was declared in 1945 and recognised in 1949 after struggle. The republic has since built a unified nation across its vast archipelago.",
    cult: "Hundreds of ethnic groups and languages coexist under the motto 'Unity in Diversity'. Islam is the majority faith, with Christianity, Hinduism and Buddhism also present. Batik, wayang puppetry, gamelan music and Balinese dance are treasured arts. Rendang, satay and nasi goreng are beloved dishes, and Bali's temples and beaches are world-famous.",
    facts: [["Area", "1.9M km²"], ["Population", "~278M"], ["Capital", "Jakarta"], ["Currency", "Rupiah"], ["Language", "Indonesian"]]
  },
  "484": {
    tags: ["Mesoamerican civilisation", "Bilingual nation", "Vibrant culture"],
    geo: "Mexico lies in southern North America, about 1.96 million square kilometres, bordered by the US to the north and the Gulf of Mexico and Pacific to the east and west. The central plateau and the Sierra Madre ranges dominate, with the Yucatán peninsula in the southeast. Popocatépetl is an active volcano. Climates range from arid north to tropical south, giving great ecological variety.",
    hist: "Mesoamerican civilisations—Olmec, Maya and Aztec—built great cities and advanced astronomy and mathematics. The Aztec Empire fell to Spanish conquistadors in 1521, beginning three centuries of colonial rule and cultural blending. Independence was won in 1821, followed by political upheaval and the Mexican-American War. The 1910 revolution shaped modern Mexico, which is now a major economy within NAFTA/USMCA.",
    cult: "Mexican culture fuses Indigenous and Spanish heritage. Día de los Muertos, with its colorful altars, is a treasured tradition. Mariachi music, folk dance and the iconic sombrero are celebrated worldwide. Tacos, mole and tequila are beloved; the pyramids of Teotihuacán and Chichén Itzá testify to a rich pre-Hispanic past.",
    facts: [["Area", "1.96M km²"], ["Population", "~129M"], ["Capital", "Mexico City"], ["Currency", "Peso"], ["Language", "Spanish"]]
  },
  "032": {
    tags: ["Second largest in South America", "Pampas plains", "Tango nation"],
    geo: "Argentina occupies most of the southern cone of South America, about 2.78 million square kilometres. The Andes run along the west, the fertile Pampas plains stretch across the centre, and Patagonia extends to the south and east; the Río de la Plata forms a broad estuary. The climate varies from subtropical in the north to subpolar in the south, with strong winds across the open plains.",
    hist: "Indigenous peoples inhabited the land before Spanish colonisation in the 16th century, centred on the Río de la Plata. The United Provinces of the Río de la Plata declared independence in 1816. The 19th century saw civil conflict, expansion and economic growth built on agriculture and beef exports. The 20th century brought industrialisation, periods of military rule and a return to democracy in 1983.",
    cult: "Tango, born in Buenos Aires, is an iconic cultural expression; the city is a cosmopolitan centre of literature, theatre and design. Asado barbecue, empanadas and Malbec wine are celebrated. The country is passionate about football—home to Lionel Messi and World Cup triumphs. The vast landscapes of Patagonia and the Andes draw adventurers worldwide.",
    facts: [["Area", "2.78M km²"], ["Population", "~46M"], ["Capital", "Buenos Aires"], ["Currency", "Argentine Peso"], ["Language", "Spanish"]]
  },
  "152": {
    tags: ["Long narrow country", "Andes & Pacific", "Copper nation"],
    geo: "Chile stretches along South America's western edge, about 756,000 square kilometres, a long narrow strip between the Andes and the Pacific. The Atacama Desert in the north is one of the world's driest places; central valleys have a Mediterranean climate, while the south has fjords, glaciers and temperate rainforests. The country also includes Easter Island far out in the Pacific.",
    hist: "Indigenous Mapuche and other peoples lived here before Inca expansion into the north. Spanish conquistadors arrived in the 1540s and ruled for three centuries. Independence was won in 1818 under Bernardo O'Higgins. The 19th century brought the War of the Pacific and territorial expansion; a nitrate and copper boom fuelled growth. A 1973 coup led to military rule until democracy returned in 1990.",
    cult: "Chilean culture blends Indigenous, Spanish and immigrant influences. Folk music and the cueca dance are traditional; the country is renowned for wine, seafood and the famed blue skies. Easter Island's giant moai statues are a world heritage mystery. Football, tennis and a love of the outdoors reflect a vibrant national spirit.",
    facts: [["Area", "756K km²"], ["Population", "~20M"], ["Capital", "Santiago"], ["Currency", "Chilean Peso"], ["Language", "Spanish"]]
  },
  "792": {
    tags: ["Bridge between continents", "Ottoman legacy", "Crossroads of civilisations"],
    geo: "Türkiye straddles Europe and Asia across the Bosporus, about 783,000 square kilometres, bordered by the Black, Aegean and Mediterranean Seas. The Anatolian plateau rises in the centre, ringed by mountains; the Bosphorus and Dardanelles link the Black Sea to the Mediterranean. A temperate climate with Mediterranean coasts and continental interior supports diverse landscapes.",
    hist: "Anatolia hosted Hittite, Greek, Roman and Byzantine civilisations. The Seljuk Turks arrived in the 11th century, followed by the Ottomans who founded an empire spanning three continents; Constantinople fell in 1453. After WWI the Ottoman Empire ended and the Republic of Türkiye was founded in 1923 under Mustafa Kemal Atatürk, who launched sweeping secular modernisation.",
    cult: "Türkiye blends Turkish, Ottoman, Greek and Middle Eastern traditions. Istanbul, spanning two continents, is a cultural crossroads. Turkish baths, whirling dervishes, carpets and the unique coffee culture are famed. Cuisine—kebabs, meze and baklava—is world-renowned; tea and hospitality are central social rituals.",
    facts: [["Area", "783K km²"], ["Population", "~85M"], ["Capital", "Ankara"], ["Currency", "Turkish Lira"], ["Language", "Turkish"]]
  },
  "364": {
    tags: ["Ancient Persian civilisation", "Plateau nation", "Silk Road hub"],
    geo: "Iran lies on the Iranian Plateau in West Asia, about 1.65 million square kilometres. The Zagros and Alborz mountains frame vast central deserts; Mount Damavand is the highest peak in West Asia. The climate is mostly arid with cold winters and hot summers. Oil and gas reserves are immense, and the land sits at the crossroads of Central Asia, the Middle East and the Persian Gulf.",
    hist: "Persia's Achaemenid Empire, founded by Cyrus the Great, was one of history's largest, followed by Parthian and Sasanian dynasties. The Arab conquest in the 7th century brought Islam; Persia nonetheless kept a distinct language and culture. The Safavids made Shia Islam the state religion in the 16th century. The Qajar dynasty gave way to the Pahlavis, and the 1979 Islamic Revolution established the current republic.",
    cult: "Persian culture is ancient and refined—poets such as Ferdowsi, Hafez and Rumi are beloved worldwide. The Persian carpet, miniature painting and intricate tilework are celebrated arts. Nowruz, the Persian New Year, is widely observed. Cuisine features rice dishes, kebabs and saffron; tea houses and hospitality are central to social life.",
    facts: [["Area", "1.65M km²"], ["Population", "~89M"], ["Capital", "Tehran"], ["Currency", "Rial"], ["Language", "Persian"]]
  },
  "682": {
    tags: ["Arabian Peninsula", "Oil-rich kingdom", "Desert nation"],
    geo: "Saudi Arabia occupies most of the Arabian Peninsula in West Asia, about 2.15 million square kilometres. Vast deserts, including the Rub' al-Khali, dominate the landscape, with mountains in the west and the Empty Quarter in the south. There are no permanent rivers. A hot desert climate brings scorching summers and cooler winters; the country holds some of the world's largest oil reserves.",
    hist: "The Arabian Peninsula was home to ancient trade routes and the Nabataean kingdom. Islam arose in the 7th century in Mecca and Medina, and the peninsula became the faith's heartland. The Al Saud dynasty unified much of the region in the 18th and 19th centuries; the modern kingdom was proclaimed in 1932. Oil discovered in the 1930s transformed the economy and society.",
    cult: "As the birthplace of Islam, Saudi Arabia is home to the holy cities of Mecca and Medina and the annual Hajj pilgrimage. Bedouin heritage, Arabic poetry and the Ardah dance are treasured. Coffee ceremonies and dates are symbols of hospitality. The kingdom is investing heavily in education, tourism and Vision 2030 to diversify beyond oil.",
    facts: [["Area", "2.15M km²"], ["Population", "~37M"], ["Capital", "Riyadh"], ["Currency", "Riyal"], ["Language", "Arabic"]]
  },
  "566": {
    tags: ["Most populous in Africa", "Cultural mosaic", "Nigerian movies"],
    geo: "Nigeria lies on the Gulf of Guinea in West Africa, about 924,000 square kilometres. The Niger and Benue rivers cross and divide the land; the south has tropical rainforest and mangrove coast, the north grades into savanna and semi-desert near the Sahara. A tropical climate with distinct wet and dry seasons supports agriculture and rich oil resources in the Niger Delta.",
    hist: "Ancient Nok culture and the Yoruba, Igbo and Hausa kingdoms flourished long before European contact. The transatlantic slave trade and later British colonisation shaped the region; the Royal Niger Company and then the colony of Nigeria were established in the 19th and early 20th centuries. Independence came in 1960, followed by civil war and periods of military rule before a return to democracy in 1999.",
    cult: "Nigeria is remarkably diverse with over 500 languages; English is official while Yoruba, Igbo and Hausa are widely spoken. Nollywood produces films prolifically, and Afrobeats music has global reach. Football is a national passion. Festivals, masquerades and vibrant textiles reflect deep local traditions; jollof rice and suya are beloved dishes.",
    facts: [["Area", "924K km²"], ["Population", "~223M"], ["Capital", "Abuja"], ["Currency", "Naira"], ["Language", "English"]]
  },
  "404": {
    tags: ["Wildlife haven", "Rift Valley", "Cradle of humanity"],
    geo: "Kenya lies in East Africa on the equator, about 580,000 square kilometres, bordered by the Indian Ocean and the Great Rift Valley. The landscape rises from a hot coastal plain through highlands and savanna to Mount Kenya, the second highest peak in Africa. The climate varies with altitude; savanna ecosystems support famous wildlife migrations. Lake Victoria and the Tana are key waters.",
    hist: "The Rift Valley in Kenya holds some of the oldest human fossils, earning it a place as a cradle of humanity. Cushitic, Nilotic and Bantu peoples migrated into the region over millennia. Swahili city-states traded along the coast from the 8th century. British colonisation began in the late 19th century with the building of the railway; independence came in 1963 under Jomo Kenyatta.",
    cult: "Over forty ethnic groups enrich Kenya's culture; Swahili and English are official. The Maasai, with their distinctive dress and cattle culture, are iconic. Safari, the Great Migration in the Maasai Mara and Amboseli's elephants are world-famous. Athletics, especially long-distance running, is a source of national pride; tea and coffee are major products.",
    facts: [["Area", "580K km²"], ["Population", "~55M"], ["Capital", "Nairobi"], ["Currency", "Shilling"], ["Language", "Swahili, English"]]
  },
  "804": {
    tags: ["Eastern Europe", "Fertile black soil", "Independent republic"],
    geo: "Ukraine lies in Eastern Europe, about 604,000 square kilometres, bordered by the Black Sea and the Sea of Azov to the south. Most of the land is rolling plain with the famous fertile black-earth belt; the Dnieper divides the country and the Carpathians rise in the west. A temperate continental climate supports abundant grain fields, earning it a reputation as Europe's breadbasket.",
    hist: "Kievan Rus was a powerful medieval state centred on Kyiv. After Mongol invasion the region came under Lithuanian, Polish and later Russian influence. The 17th-century Cossack uprising and the 1654 union with Russia reshaped its fate. Part of the Russian Empire and then the USSR, Ukraine endured famine and war in the 20th century. It declared independence in 1991 as the Soviet Union collapsed.",
    cult: "Ukrainian culture blends Slavic traditions with a distinct identity; Ukrainian and Russian are widely spoken. Folk song, dance and the embroidered rushnyk are cherished. Borscht, varenyky and sunflower oil are staple foods. Kyiv's golden-domed churches and the historic centres of Lviv and Odesa reflect a rich architectural heritage.",
    facts: [["Area", "604K km²"], ["Population", "~37M"], ["Capital", "Kyiv"], ["Currency", "Hryvnia"], ["Language", "Ukrainian"]]
  },
  "408": {
    tags: ["Hermit kingdom", "Peninsula north", "Socialist state"],
    geo: "North Korea occupies the northern half of the Korean Peninsula, about 121,000 square kilometres, bordered by China and Russia and facing the Sea of Japan and the Yellow Sea. Mountainous and highland terrain dominates, with the Taebaek range; the Yalu and Tumen rivers form the northern boundary. A temperate continental climate brings cold winters and warm summers, with rugged, forested landscapes.",
    hist: "Korean kingdoms and the Goryeo and Joseon dynasties shaped the peninsula for centuries. After Japan's colonial rule ended in 1945, the peninsula was divided along the 38th parallel. The Democratic People's Republic of Korea was founded in 1948. The Korean War in the 1950s ended in an armistice, after which the north developed as a centrally planned, isolated state.",
    cult: "Korean traditions such as Confucian-influenced etiquette, folk dance and the distinct hanbok remain important. Mass games and monumental architecture express state ideology. Naengmyeon and kimchi are common foods. The DMZ and Mount Kumgang mark a land of striking mountains and a strongly collective social life.",
    facts: [["Area", "121K km²"], ["Population", "~26M"], ["Capital", "Pyongyang"], ["Currency", "Won"], ["Language", "Korean"]]
  },
  "586": {
    tags: ["South Asian giant", "Indus civilisation", "Mountain & plains"],
    geo: "Pakistan lies in South Asia at the crossroads of Central and West Asia, about 796,000 square kilometres. The northern mountains include the Karakoram and K2, the world's second highest peak; the Indus River flows south through fertile plains to the Arabian Sea. Balochistan's plateaus and deserts lie in the west. The climate is largely arid with hot summers and a monsoon season.",
    hist: "The Indus Valley civilisation, with cities like Mohenjo-daro, flourished over 4,000 years ago. The region saw Persian, Greek, Mauryan, Kushan and Gupta rule before the arrival of Islam in the 8th century. The Mughal Empire and later the Sikh and British eras followed. Pakistan was created in 1947 as a homeland for South Asian Muslims, becoming a republic in 1956.",
    cult: "Pakistan is a mainly Muslim country with deep-rooted traditions; Urdu and English are official, with Punjabi, Sindhi and Pashto widely used. Cricket is the most beloved sport. Biryani, kebabs and curry are central to its cuisine; ghazal poetry and qawwali music are treasured. The badshahi mosque in Lahore and the ancient sites of the Indus reflect a layered heritage.",
    facts: [["Area", "796K km²"], ["Population", "~245M"], ["Capital", "Islamabad"], ["Currency", "Rupee"], ["Language", "Urdu, English"]]
  },
  "458": {
    tags: ["Maritime Southeast Asia", "Straits crossroads", "Multi-ethnic"],
    geo: "Malaysia lies in Southeast Asia, about 330,000 square kilometres, split between the Malay Peninsula and northern Borneo by the South China Sea. The peninsula has a central mountain range and coastal plains; Borneo's part is hilly and densely forested. A tropical rainforest climate brings year-round heat and heavy rain. The Strait of Malacca is one of the world's busiest shipping lanes.",
    hist: "Early Hindu-Buddhist kingdoms and the Malacca Sultanate made the region a trading hub from the 15th century. The Portuguese, Dutch and British successively controlled the straits; British Malaya and North Borneo were later crown colonies. Japanese occupation during WWII was followed by independence: the Federation of Malaya in 1957 and Malaysia in 1963.",
    cult: "Malay, Chinese and Indian communities create a rich multicultural fabric; Islam is the official religion. Nasi lemak, satay and laksa are popular dishes; batik and songket textiles are traditional crafts. The Petronas Twin Towers in Kuala Lumpur symbolise modern growth, while Borneo's rainforests and beaches draw nature lovers.",
    facts: [["Area", "330K km²"], ["Population", "~34M"], ["Capital", "Kuala Lumpur"], ["Currency", "Ringgit"], ["Language", "Malay"]]
  },
  "608": {
    tags: ["Archipelago nation", "Western Pacific", "Trait d'union"],
    geo: "The Philippines is an archipelago of over 7,000 islands in the western Pacific, about 300,000 square kilometres. Luzon, Mindanao and the Visayas are the main island groups. Volcanic and mountainous, the country lies on the Pacific Ring of Fire and faces frequent earthquakes and typhoons. A tropical maritime climate brings hot, wet conditions; rice terraces and coral reefs are notable.",
    hist: "Indigenous barangays existed before Spanish explorers arrived in 1521; the Philippines became a Spanish colony named after King Philip II. Three centuries of Spanish rule spread Catholicism and a Latin influence. The 1896 revolution sought independence, but the US acquired the islands in 1898. Independence finally came in 1946, after Japanese occupation in WWII.",
    cult: "A blend of Malay, Spanish and American influences defines Filipino culture; English and Filipino are official and Catholicism is widespread. Adobo, sinigang and halo-halo are beloved dishes. Jeepneys, fiestas and the tinikling dance are icons. The Banaue rice terraces and the volcanic island of Mayon reflect a striking natural and cultural landscape.",
    facts: [["Area", "300K km²"], ["Population", "~117M"], ["Capital", "Manila"], ["Currency", "Peso"], ["Language", "Filipino, English"]]
  },
  "554": {
    tags: ["Island nation", "Southern Pacific", "Alpine & geothermal"],
    geo: "New Zealand lies in the southwest Pacific, about 268,000 square kilometres, made up of the North and South Islands plus smaller islands. The Southern Alps run down the South Island with glaciers and fjords, while the North Island has volcanic peaks and geothermal fields. A temperate maritime climate brings mild, wet weather. Its isolation produced unique wildlife such as the kiwi.",
    hist: "Polynesian Māori settlers arrived around the 13th century and built a distinctive culture. Dutch explorer Abel Tasman sighted the islands in 1642; Captain Cook mapped them in the 1760s, after which British settlement began. The Treaty of Waitangi of 1840 established British sovereignty while protecting Māori rights. New Zealand gained full independence through the Statute of Westminster in 1947.",
    cult: "Māori culture—with the haka, carving and the poi—is integral to national identity; English and Māori are official. A love of the outdoors, rugby and adventure sports defines the lifestyle. Sauvignon blanc wine, lamb and pavlova are famed. Queenstown's mountains and Rotorua's geysers showcase a dramatic landscape.",
    facts: [["Area", "268K km²"], ["Population", "~5.2M"], ["Capital", "Wellington"], ["Currency", "NZ Dollar"], ["Language", "English, Māori"]]
  },
  "496": {
    tags: ["Landlocked plateau", "Steppe nation", "Gobi Desert"],
    geo: "Mongolia is a landlocked country in East Asia between Russia and China, about 1.57 million square kilometres. The landscape rises from the Gobi Desert in the south through vast steppes to mountains in the north and west. A harsh continental climate brings extreme temperature swings and low rainfall. Grasslands cover much of the land, supporting a traditional pastoral way of life.",
    hist: "The Mongol Empire, founded by Genghis Khan in the 13th century, became the largest contiguous empire in history, linking East and West along the Silk Road. After its fragmentation, Mongolia fell under Qing rule in the 17th century. Following the Qing collapse, it declared independence in 1911 and solidified it in 1921, later becoming a socialist state aligned with the USSR. A peaceful revolution in 1990 ushered in democracy and a market economy.",
    cult: "Nomadic heritage is central: the ger (yurt), horse riding and the Naadam festival with wrestling, racing and archery are iconic. Buddhism and shamanic traditions coexist. Throat singing and the morin khuur (horse-head fiddle) are treasured arts. The vast steppe, the Gobi and the flute-like landscapes evoke a deep connection to nature.",
    facts: [["Area", "1.57M km²"], ["Population", "~3.5M"], ["Capital", "Ulaanbaatar"], ["Currency", "Tögrög"], ["Language", "Mongolian"]]
  },
  "616": {
    tags: ["Central European plain", "Slavic nation", "EU member"],
    geo: "Poland lies in Central Europe, about 313,000 square kilometres, stretching from the Baltic Sea in the north to the Carpathians and Sudetes in the south. Most of the land is a broad lowland plain crossed by the Vistula and Oder rivers. A temperate climate with continental influences brings warm summers and cold winters. Fertile soils and forests shape the landscape.",
    hist: " Slavic tribes unified under the Piast dynasty; Poland was Christianised in 966 and became a kingdom in 1025. The 16th century was a golden age, and the Polish-Lithuanian Commonwealth was a major European power. The 18th-century partitions erased the state until 1918. After WWII borders shifted west and a communist regime ruled until the Solidarity movement and free elections in 1989. Poland joined the EU in 2004.",
    cult: "Polish culture is deeply Catholic and Slavic; Chopin's music and Mickiewicz's poetry are national treasures. Pierogi, bigos and żurek are hearty staples. The historic centres of Kraków and Warsaw, reconstructed after wartime destruction, reflect resilience. Castles, folk crafts and a love of skiing and football are part of modern life.",
    facts: [["Area", "313K km²"], ["Population", "~37M"], ["Capital", "Warsaw"], ["Currency", "Złoty"], ["Language", "Polish"]]
  },
  "528": {
    tags: ["Low Countries", "Canals & dikes", "Trade hub"],
    geo: "The Netherlands lies on the North Sea in Western Europe, about 42,000 square kilometres. Much of the land is low-lying delta plain protected by dunes, dikes and the famous Delta Works; the Rhine, Meuse and Scheldt cross it. A temperate maritime climate brings mild, wet weather. Tulip fields, canals and windmills define the countryside, and Rotterdam is Europe's largest port.",
    hist: "Part of the Frankish empire and later the Holy Roman Empire, the Dutch provinces united in the Union of Utrecht in 1579 and declared independence from Spain in 1581. The Dutch Republic of the 17th century was a golden age of trade, science and art, with a vast colonial empire. It was conquered by France in the early 19th century; the modern kingdom was established in 1815 and remained neutral in WWI.",
    cult: "Dutch culture is pragmatic and cosmopolitan; Rembrandt and Van Gogh are towering artistic figures. Cycling, canals and canal-side cafés are part of daily life. Cheese, herring and stroopwafels are favourites. Amsterdam, with its museums and liberal spirit, and the Keukenhof tulip gardens draw visitors worldwide.",
    facts: [["Area", "42K km²"], ["Population", "~18M"], ["Capital", "Amsterdam"], ["Currency", "Euro"], ["Language", "Dutch"]]
  },
  "756": {
    tags: ["Alpine nation", "Neutrality", "Financial centre"],
    geo: "Switzerland is a landlocked country in Central Europe, about 41,000 square kilometres. The Alps dominate the south and centre, the Jura mountains lie in the northwest and the Swiss Plateau in between; the Rhine, Rhône and Inn rise here. A temperate altitude climate varies with elevation. Glaciers, lakes and mountain valleys create spectacular scenery.",
    hist: "Inhabited by Celtic and Germanic peoples, the region came under Roman and then Frankish rule. The Old Swiss Confederacy formed in 1291 around three cantons and expanded over centuries. The 1648 Peace of Westphalia recognised Swiss independence. A short French-controlled Helvetic Republic gave way to the federal state in 1848. Switzerland stayed neutral in both world wars and hosts many international organisations.",
    cult: "Four language regions—German, French, Italian and Romansh—create a distinct multilingual identity. Direct democracy is a hallmark. Chocolate, cheese, watches and the Alps are world-famous; skiing, hiking and the railways are beloved. Zurich and Geneva are global financial and diplomatic centres, while the Matterhorn symbolises the nation.",
    facts: [["Area", "41K km²"], ["Population", "~9M"], ["Capital", "Bern"], ["Currency", "Swiss Franc"], ["Language", "German, French, Italian, Romansh"]]
  },
  "300": {
    tags: ["Cradle of democracy", "Mediterranean", "Island nation"],
    geo: "Greece lies in southeastern Europe on the Balkan Peninsula, about 132,000 square kilometres, plus thousands of islands in the Aegean and Ionian Seas. Mountainous terrain covers most of the mainland, with fertile valleys and a deeply indented coast. A Mediterranean climate brings hot dry summers and mild wet winters. The many islands and ancient ruins define its character.",
    hist: "Ancient Greece flourished from the 8th century BCE with city-states such as Athens and Sparta; it gave the world democracy, philosophy, theatre and the Olympic Games. The Persian Wars and the Peloponnesian War shaped the classical era, followed by the conquests of Philip II and Alexander the Great. Roman, Byzantine and Ottoman rule followed in turn. Modern Greece won independence from the Ottomans in 1830.",
    cult: "Greek heritage—philosophy, the Parthenon, mythology and the Olympics—deeply shaped Western civilisation. Greek Orthodox Christianity is central. Rebetiko and bouzouki music, syrtaki dance, feta, olives and moussaka are beloved. The islands, with Santorini and Mykonos, and the legacy of Plato and Aristotle make Greece timeless.",
    facts: [["Area", "132K km²"], ["Population", "~10M"], ["Capital", "Athens"], ["Currency", "Euro"], ["Language", "Greek"]]
  },
  "376": {
    tags: ["Ancient land", "Start-up nation", "Desert & innovation"],
    geo: "Israel lies on the eastern Mediterranean in West Asia, about 22,000 square kilometres. The coastal plain sits beside the Mediterranean, the central hills rise toward Jerusalem, the Jordan Rift Valley runs east and the Negev Desert spreads south. A Mediterranean climate in the north grades to arid desert in the south. Water is scarce and carefully managed.",
    hist: "The land is central to Jewish, Christian and Muslim heritage and was home to the ancient kingdoms of Israel and Judah. It passed through Assyrian, Babylonian, Persian, Greek, Roman and Byzantine rule, then Arab and Ottoman control. The modern State of Israel was established in 1948, followed by wars and peace treaties with neighbouring countries. It has since become a regional economic and technological leader.",
    cult: "Israel is a melting pot of Jewish communities from around the world and a large Arab minority; Hebrew and Arabic are official. Innovation and entrepreneurship thrive—the 'Start-Up Nation'. Hummus, falafel and shakshuka are staples. Jerusalem, Tel Aviv and the Dead Sea combine ancient sanctity with modern dynamism.",
    facts: [["Area", "22K km²"], ["Population", "~10M"], ["Capital", "Jerusalem"], ["Currency", "New Shekel"], ["Language", "Hebrew, Arabic"]]
  },
  "192": {
    tags: ["Caribbean island", "Revolutionary history", "Salsa & cigars"],
    geo: "Cuba is the largest island in the Caribbean, about 110,000 square kilometres, lying south of Florida and east of Mexico's Yucatán. The island is mostly low rolling plains with the Sierra Maestra mountains in the southeast and fertile valleys. A tropical maritime climate brings warm temperatures, a wet season and the threat of hurricanes. Coral reefs and white-sand beaches fringe the coast.",
    hist: "Indigenous Taíno and Ciboney peoples lived here before Columbus arrived in 1492; Spain colonised the island for centuries, using it as a hub of the Caribbean empire. The Cuban War of Independence and the Spanish-American War of 1898 ended Spanish rule. The 1959 revolution led by Fidel Castro established a socialist state, which endured a US embargo and became a Cold War focal point.",
    cult: "Cuban culture blends Spanish, African and Indigenous heritage. Salsa, son, rumba and the danzón are iconic; cigars, rum and classic cars are famed. Baseball is the national sport. Havana's colourful streets, colonial architecture and the legacy of Che Guevara make the island a powerful cultural symbol.",
    facts: [["Area", "110K km²"], ["Population", "~11M"], ["Capital", "Havana"], ["Currency", "Cuban Peso"], ["Language", "Spanish"]]
  },
  "158": {
    tags: ["Part of China", "Western Pacific island", "Treasure Island"],
    geo: "Taiwan, a province of China, lies off the southeastern coast of the Chinese mainland on the continental shelf, facing the Pacific to the east and the Taiwan Strait to the west. It comprises the main island plus the Penghu, Diaoyu and other islands, about 36,000 square kilometres. The Central Range runs north-south with Yushan reaching 3,952 metres; western plains are fertile. Subtropical and tropical monsoons bring abundant rain.",
    hist: "Taiwan has been Chinese territory since ancient times: Wu envoys reached it in the Three Kingdoms era and the Song and Yuan dynasties set up administrative bodies in Penghu. Dutch and Spanish colonists occupied parts in the 17th century until Zheng Chenggong recovered the island in 1662. The Qing set up a prefecture in 1684 and a province in 1885. It was occupied by Japan in 1895 and returned to China after victory in 1945.",
    cult: "Taiwanese culture is an integral part of Chinese culture, blending Minnan and Hakka traditions with indigenous influences. Mandarin, Minnan and Hakka are spoken. Temple festivals and traditional customs remain vibrant. The National Palace Museum houses vast imperial collections, while Alishan, Sun Moon Lake and Taroko Gorge are celebrated landscapes; tea and night-market snacks are beloved.",
    facts: [["Part of", "China"], ["Area", "~36K km²"], ["Population", "~23M"], ["Capital", "Taipei"], ["Currency", "New Taiwan Dollar"]]
  }
};
